import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={radioactivity:{theory:{concept:"[[Radioactivity is the spontaneous decay of unstable atomic nuclei, during which they emit radiation.]] When exactly a single nucleus decays is pure chance \u2014 nobody can predict it. Yet a large number of identical nuclei behaves very regularly: in every equal stretch of time the same fraction of the remaining nuclei decays. The time in which half of the nuclei decay is called the half-life; after n half-lives, 1/2\u207F of the nuclei remain."},section:{atom:{title:"Unstable nuclei",text:"A nucleus is held together by the strong force, but some combinations of protons and neutrons are not stable. Such a nucleus changes into another one and throws out radiation: an \u03B1-particle (a helium nucleus), a \u03B2-particle (an electron, or its antiparticle the positron) or a \u03B3-ray (very energetic light). The nucleus that is left is called the daughter nucleus. In the picture every large solid dot is an unstable nucleus; when it decays it shrinks to a small faint dot and a flash marks the moment."},chance:{title:"Decay is chance",text:"A nucleus has no clock and no memory. An atom that has been waiting for a million years is exactly as likely to decay in the next second as one that was just made. Nothing can speed it up or slow it down. [[So the decay time of a single atom cannot be predicted \u2014 you can only give the probability.]] Try a sample of 16 atoms and run it several times (each run takes the next experiment number): the results differ every time!"},halfLife:{title:"Half-life",text:"[[The half-life T\xBD is the time in which each nucleus has a 50 % chance to decay \u2014 so about half of a large sample decays.]] It is a property of the isotope: technetium-99m, which doctors use, has 6 hours; iodine-131 has 8 days; carbon-14 has 5730 years; uranium-238 has 4.5 billion years \u2014 about the age of the Earth. You cannot say when a given atom decays, but you can say very precisely when half of a million atoms are gone."},law:{title:"The law of decay",text:"After one half-life \xBD of the atoms are left, after two \xBC, after three \u215B \u2014 each time the remaining number is halved again: N = N\u2080 \xB7 2^(\u2212t/T\xBD). It never reaches zero exactly: after 10 half-lives about a thousandth is left (\xBD to the tenth power). On a graph this is a smooth curve that falls fast at first and then slowly; if you plot the logarithm of N, you get a straight line."},activity:{title:"Activity",text:"The activity A is the number of decays per second (measured in becquerels). Because each nucleus has the same chance, the activity is proportional to the number of nuclei left: A = \u03BB \xB7 N, where \u03BB = ln 2 / T\xBD. So the activity falls by half every half-life too. A Geiger counter clicks at this rate. With few atoms the counts jump around; with many they follow the smooth curve."},uses:{title:"Using half-lives",text:"Living things take in carbon-14 from the air; when they die, no new carbon-14 comes in and the amount halves every 5730 years \u2014 so measuring what is left tells the age of a bone or a wooden tool (up to about 50 000 years). Uranium-238 dates rocks and the Earth itself. Doctors inject a little technetium-99m: it shines gamma rays for a few hours so that organs can be photographed, and then it is gone from the body."},danger:{title:"Short or long half-life?",text:"A short half-life means that atoms decay quickly: the sample is very active now, but the danger disappears soon. A long half-life means that decays are rare: the activity is weak, but it lasts for thousands of years \u2014 which is why long-lived waste needs to be stored carefully for a very long time. The half-life alone does not tell how dangerous a material is; it also depends on the kind of radiation and on the amount."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A sample has 800 atoms. How many are left after 3 half-lives? (About 100.) Does a sample with 1 atom left lose it in the next half-life for sure? (No \u2014 it has a 50 % chance.) A bone has \xBC of its carbon-14. How old is it? (2 half-lives = 11 460 years.) Why does the bar not end exactly at the marker line (the law)? (Chance \u2014 it matters more with fewer atoms.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{law:"The law of decay: what is left after time t (T\xBD is the half-life, N\u2080 the number at the start)",activity:"Activity: decays per unit of time, proportional to the number of atoms left",constant:"The decay constant: the chance of one atom decaying per unit of time",exponential:"The same law with the decay constant",meanLife:"Mean lifetime: the average time an atom survives",scatter:"Typical scatter of the number left around the expected value (chance)"},variable:{atoms:"Number of unstable atoms",halfLife:"Half-life",constant:"Decay constant",activity:"Activity (decays per second)",meanLife:"Mean lifetime"},assumption:{chain:"Every atom of one isotope is identical and turns into a stable daughter; decay chains, where the daughter decays too, are ignored.",radiation:"The emitted \u03B1, \u03B2 or \u03B3 radiation is not followed: a flash only marks the moment of decay.",small:"Not to scale: a real sample holds about 10\xB2\xB2 atoms, not a few hundred. That is why a real decay curve is perfectly smooth and ours is not.",time:"Real half-lives are compressed: the run is measured in half-lives, so uranium-238 shows 4.5 billion years in a few seconds."},param:{isotope:"Isotope",atoms:"Number of atoms",duration:"Run length (half-lives)",seed:"Experiment number",rate:"Speed (half-lives per second)",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{isotope:"The isotope sets the real half-life shown in the picture; the shape of the decay is the same for all of them.",atoms:"Few atoms: chance is clearly visible. Many atoms: the curve is smooth.",duration:"The run stops after this many half-lives.",seed:"Another number gives another random experiment with the same atoms. Each new run takes the next number; set a number back to repeat that run exactly.",rate:"How fast time runs: half-lives per second."},preset:{atoms16:"16 atoms",atoms100:"100 atoms",atoms400:"400 atoms",atoms900:"900 atoms",half1:"1 half-life",half2:"2",half3:"3",half6:"6",half10:"10"},isotope:{generic:"Generic isotope",tc99m:"Technetium-99m",i131:"Iodine-131",co60:"Cobalt-60",c14:"Carbon-14",ra226:"Radium-226",u238:"Uranium-238"},unit:{none:"",hours:"hours",days:"days",years:"years",billionYears:"billion years"},decay:{alpha:"\u03B1 decay",beta:"\u03B2 decay",gamma:"\u03B3 emission",none:""},measure:{remaining:"Unstable atoms left",percent:"Share left",halves:"Time in half-lives",decayed:"Atoms decayed",expected:"Expected by the law",deviation:"Difference from the law",activity:"Expected activity (decays per half-life)",observedActivity:"Counted activity (decays per half-life)",sigma:"Typical scatter (\u03C3)",deviationPercent:"Difference, share of all atoms",meanLife:"Mean lifetime (in half-lives)",logRemaining:"ln of the atoms left",logExpected:"ln of the expected number"},view:{genericLine:"Generic isotope \xB7 half-life T\xBD",isotopeLine:"{name} \xB7 half-life {halfLife} {unit} \xB7 {decay}",count:"Left: {remaining} of {total} \xB7 expected by the law: {expected}",time:"t = {real} {unit} = {halves} half-lives",genericTime:"t = {halves} half-lives",flashesOption:"Flashes at decay",daughtersOption:"Show decayed atoms"},graph:{decay:"Atoms left against time (dashed: the law)",log:"Logarithm of the atoms left (a straight line)",activity:"Activity against time"},challenge:{halfLeft:{title:"Half is left",description:"Run for exactly 1 half-life with at least 100 atoms, so that about half of the atoms (40\u201360 %) are left.",hint:"Set the run length to 1 half-life and wait for the run to end."},quarter:{title:"A quarter is left",description:"Run for exactly 2 half-lives with at least 100 atoms, so that about a quarter (17\u201333 %) is left.",hint:"Half of a half is a quarter. Set the run length to 2 half-lives."},luck:{title:"Chance in a small sample",description:"In a sample of 25 atoms or fewer, run 1 half-life so that the number left differs from the expected value by at least 10 % of all atoms.",hint:"Take 16 atoms. If the result is too close to half, simply run it again: each run is a new random experiment."},carbon:{title:"Radiocarbon dating",description:"A wooden tool has about a quarter of its carbon-14 left. Choose carbon-14 and run 2 half-lives with at least 200 atoms. How many years are these?",hint:"Two half-lives of carbon-14 are 2 \xD7 5730 years \u2014 read the time under the picture."},tracer:{title:"A tracer fades",description:"A hospital injects technetium-99m. After 4 half-lives (a day) only a few per cent are left: run it with at least 200 atoms.",hint:"Choose technetium-99m and set the run length to 4 half-lives."},bigSample:{title:"Large numbers smooth chance",description:"With 900 atoms, run 1 half-life so that the number left is within 3 % of all atoms of the value predicted by the law.",hint:"Use the maximum number of atoms. The scatter is about 1.7 % of 900; if it is bigger, try another experiment number."}}}},i={sim:a};export{i as default,a as sim};
